    {
      "id": "reader_unit_017",
      "section_kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "section_title": "Interpretive Questions",
      "text": "Acts presents women as disciples, prophets, patrons, hosts, household leaders, teachers, workers, converts, and sufferers, though narration remains selective and patriarchal constraints persist. Matthias is selected by prayer, scriptural interpretation, nomination, and lot to restore the Twelve after Judas. Acts does not securely identify its place of composition or a single city of origin. Acts 1 explicitly resumes a former account addressed to Theophilus and is most naturally read as a sequel to Luke. Theophilus is named as addressee, but whether he was a patron, official, convert, representative reader, or literary dedicatee is unknown. Acts narrates Jesus' ascent, a cloud, and angelic interpretation, drawing on biblical theophanic imagery rather than supplying a modern physical mechanism. Common authorship of Luke and Acts remains the majority scholarly judgment, though the exact composition and publication relationship is debated. Acts participates in ancient historiographic narrative while also inviting comparison with biography, institutional origins, travel, and apologetic writing. The disciples ask about restoring the kingdom to Israel; Jesus redirects timing toward Spirit-empowered witness without declaring Israel irrelevant. Later Christian tradition identifies the author as Luke, Paul's physician companion, but Acts itself does not make that identification.",
      "verse_refs": [
        "Acts 1:14",
        "Acts 1:1",
        "Acts 1:1-2",
